    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e11fd4f8-e2f2-5d81-82cb-aad210a5c75e",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=e^{y}$\n- **II.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{x}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{\u2032}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=e^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=y^{2}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "467fdc78-06fc-5c47-a755-f9a550d94510",
