      ":question/worked-solution": "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{\u2032}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\sqrt{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
